<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    ============== the automated medical suite in pulse and recoil armour =======

    Both suits carry a trauma package in the books: they seal punctures, compress
    wounds, stop bleeding and push combat stims when the wearer is badly hurt.
    Neither one heals anybody. Recovery in that setting is bio-gel, a Carver and
    a lot of time, so nothing here restores tissue - these defs stabilise a
    casualty and manage pain, and that is all.

    WHY THIS IS TWO SEAL DEFS AND NOT ONE. HediffSet.CalculateBleedRate, decompiled,
    multiplies every stage's totalBleedFactor together. Two defs therefore stack
    correctly and independently if a pawn somehow wears both, and each comp only
    ever has to manage its own def. One shared def with the comp setting severity
    would have to reconcile two writers against a single hediff instance, which is
    the kind of thing that survives testing and then breaks on a load.

    RECOIL SEALS BETTER THAN PULSE, DELIBERATELY. The recoil-plates are described
    as mechanically coiling around a wound to compress the tissue, which is a
    better description of haemostasis than anything pulse armour does; pulse
    armour's advantage is its response suite, so it carries more stim doses and a
    faster resupply. That gives the cheaper suit one axis it genuinely wins on. It
    is a design call rather than a fact - flip the two numbers if the flagship
    should simply be better at everything.
  -->

  <HediffDef>
    <defName>RR_TraumaSeal_Recoil</defName>
    <label>recoil-plate seal</label>
    <labelNoun>a recoil-plate seal</labelNoun>
    <description>The plating has closed on this person's wounds. Mechanical-chemical plates coil inward against torn tissue and hold it compressed while clotting agents go in behind them. It will not mend anything - it stops them emptying out while somebody who can mend it is found.</description>
    <defaultLabelColor>(0.55, 0.75, 0.85)</defaultLabelColor>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
    <maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <!--
          Precedent: Royalty's Coagulator implant, one stage, totalBleedFactor 0.5.
          The armour is considerably better than a coagulator and is also the
          reason a Peerless Scarred survives a razor fight, so 0.15 - bleeding
          continues, but slowly enough that a casualty can be dragged out.
        -->
        <totalBleedFactor>0.15</totalBleedFactor>
        <painFactor>0.85</painFactor>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

  <HediffDef>
    <defName>RR_TraumaSeal_Pulse</defName>
    <label>pulse-suit seal</label>
    <labelNoun>a pulse-suit seal</labelNoun>
    <description>The suit is treating this person without being asked. Punctures are sealed as they are made and clotting agents are pushed into the wound channel. It buys time, nothing more - the suit has no way to give back what has already been lost.</description>
    <defaultLabelColor>(0.55, 0.75, 0.85)</defaultLabelColor>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
    <maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <totalBleedFactor>0.30</totalBleedFactor>
        <painFactor>0.85</painFactor>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

  <!--
    The stim itself. Modelled on GoJuiceHigh - same shape, same fields, weaker,
    and shorter: severityPerDay -6 burns a full dose off in four hours.

    No addiction and no ChemicalDef. Vanilla couples those through a ChemicalDef
    and a Need, and a suit that quietly addicted its wearer would need a whole
    withdrawal design behind it. The cost here is that doses are finite and the
    suit takes a long time to make more.
  -->
  <HediffDef>
    <defName>RR_CombatStim</defName>
    <hediffClass>Hediff_High</hediffClass>
    <label>combat stim</label>
    <labelNoun>a combat stim</labelNoun>
    <description>A dose of battlefield stimulant, injected by armour that decided its wearer needed it. Pain goes distant and the body does what it is told for a while longer.</description>
    <defaultLabelColor>(1, 0.4, 0.4)</defaultLabelColor>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
    <maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
    <!--
      Explicit rather than left to the default. A full dose burning off at
      -6 severity per day lasts four hours; taking whatever the default happened
      to be would have made the stim's duration an accident.
    -->
    <initialSeverity>1</initialSeverity>
    <comps>
      <li Class="HediffCompProperties_SeverityPerDay">
        <severityPerDay>-6</severityPerDay>
        <showHoursToRecover>true</showHoursToRecover>
      </li>
    </comps>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <painFactor>0.2</painFactor>
        <capMods>
          <li>
            <capacity>Consciousness</capacity>
            <offset>0.15</offset>
          </li>
          <li>
            <capacity>Moving</capacity>
            <offset>0.25</offset>
          </li>
        </capMods>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

</Defs>
